<p class="sub">SHOW ME exhibit · Environmental Economics</p>
<h1>Close Seabrook. What replaces it?</h1>
<p>Seabrook Station (NH) is a <b>1,244 MW</b> nuclear plant that generates about <b>9.9 TWh a year</b>, a <b>90.7% capacity factor</b>. Wind, solar and gas run far below that, so replacing the <i>energy</i> takes much more <i>capacity</i>. Below: four ways to replace those 9.9 TWh, and what each costs in land, carbon and capital.</p>

<div class="card">
<div class="tabs" id="tabs"></div>
<div id="chartTitle" style="font-weight:bold"></div>
<div class="note" id="chartNote"></div>
<div id="chart"></div>
</div>

<div class="card w"><table id="tbl"></table></div>

<h2>Sensitivity: what the source tables say</h2>
<div class="card w"><table>
<tr><th>Swap</th><th>Basis</th><th>Result</th></tr>
<tr><td>Solar land, large PV</td><td>7.9 acres/MWac (Table 9)</td><td>~28,100 acres</td></tr>
<tr><td>Solar land, by energy</td><td>3.6 acres/GWh/yr (all PV, total)</td><td>~35,600 acres</td></tr>
<tr><td>Solar land, Northeast sun</td><td>~6 acres/GWh/yr (NREL Table 5, Newark NJ, fixed)</td><td>~59,700 acres</td></tr>
<tr><td>Wind land, total area</td><td>~84 acres/MW (34 ha/MW)</td><td>~258,000 acres</td></tr>
<tr><td>Solar capital, Northeast</td><td>$2,596/kW (EIA, NE region)</td><td>~$9.2B (vs $6.6B)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Gas capital, turbine vs engine</td><td>$841 vs $1,522/kW</td><td>$2.35B vs $4.26B</td></tr>
</table></div>
<p class="note">Land values are national averages, not New Hampshire sites. Only the Newark row is a regional estimate.</p>
<h2>How it is calculated</h2>
<pre>Capacity (MW) = annual MWh ÷ (8,760 × capacity factor)
Land (acres)  = MW × acres per MW
CO2-eq (t)    = MWh × g CO2-eq/kWh ÷ 1,000
Capital ($)   = MW × 1,000 × $/kW</pre>
<p>Example: gas = 9,900,000 ÷ (8,760 × 0.404) = 2,798 MW.</p>

<h2>Sources and assumptions</h2>
<ul class="note">
<li><b>Seabrook</b>: 1,244 MW (EIA plant capacity); 9.9 TWh/yr (GridInfo annual generation).</li>
<li><b>Capacity factors</b>: New Hampshire 2024 values (gas 40.4%, solar 31.8%, wind 36.8%), same source for all three.</li>
<li><b>Solar land</b>: 8.9 acres/MWac = <i>total-area</i>, capacity-weighted average of all U.S. utility-scale solar (PV and CSP), 2012 data. NREL/TP-6A20-56290 (Ong et al. 2013), Table ES-1 and Table 9. PV-only: 8.3 (small) and 7.9 (large).</li><li><b>Wind land</b>: 0.75 acres/MW = permanent <i>direct</i> footprint (0.3 ha/MW), NREL/TP-6A2-45834 (Denholm et al. 2009). The same study's total project area is about 34 ha/MW (~84 acres/MW). Gas: 0.343 acres/MW (EIA).</li>
<li><b>Lifecycle emissions</b>: gas 486, solar 40, wind 12 g CO₂-eq/kWh (NREL lifecycle harmonization studies; <i>not</i> the TP-6A20-56290 solar land-use report, so cite the harmonization papers directly). Seabrook's 12 g/kWh is my added placeholder for nuclear's lifecycle emissions so the comparison has a baseline. Check it against NREL's harmonized figures.</li>
<li><b>Capital cost</b>: EIA, Construction cost data for electric generators, generators installed in 2024 (Form EIA-860): all-natural-gas $1,004/kW, solar $1,865/kW, wind $1,882/kW. U.S. capacity-weighted averages, before incentives.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Read this before you trust it</h2>
<ul class="note">
<li><b>Land is not apples to apples.</b> Solar counts the whole fenced plant area; wind counts only roads and pads. On NREL's wind total-area basis, wind would need roughly 258,000 acres, not 2,300 (see sensitivity table). Wind land is heavily understated in the headline chart.</li>
<li><b>Capital cost only.</b> No fuel, O&amp;M, transmission, or backup. Gas fuel costs recur every year and solar/wind capital does not, so the cheapest build today is not the cheapest over its life.</li>
<li><b>Energy is not reliability.</b> Seabrook runs around the clock. Matching its annual TWh with solar and wind says nothing about nights, calm winter weeks, or the storage and backup needed to cover them. The firming cost is not included.</li>
<li><b>Check the inputs.</b> NH solar at 31.8% looks high for PV in New England; confirm whether it is a DC or AC figure. EIA gas $1,004/kW blends combustion turbines ($841) and engines ($1,522) and has no combined-cycle line, and U.S. averages are below Northeast costs (see below). Mixed-case gas emissions are 1,443,420 t (the working notes show a typo of 1,442,420); the total rounds to 1.64M either way.</li>
<li>Lifecycle emissions from gas depend heavily on methane leakage; 486 g is a mid-range figure.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Sources</h2>
<ol class="note">
<li>Seabrook generation (9.9 TWh/yr): GridInfo annual generation data. Plant capacity (1,244 MW): U.S. Energy Information Administration (EIA), <a href="https://www.eia.gov/electricity/generatorcosts/">eia.gov/electricity/generatorcosts</a>.</li>
<li>Ong, S., Campbell, C., Denholm, P., Margolis, R., Heath, G. (2013). <i>Land-Use Requirements for Solar Power Plants in the United States.</i> NREL/TP-6A20-56290. <a href="https://docs.nlr.gov/docs/fy13osti/56290.pdf">docs.nlr.gov/docs/fy13osti/56290.pdf</a></li>
<li>Denholm, P., Hand, M., Jackson, M., Ong, S. (2009). <i>Land-Use Requirements of Modern Wind Power Plants in the United States.</i> NREL/TP-6A2-45834. <a href="https://www.osti.gov/biblio/964608">osti.gov/biblio/964608</a></li>
<li>U.S. EIA. <i>Construction cost data for electric generators</i> (generators installed in 2024; Form EIA-860). <a href="https://www.eia.gov/electricity/generatorcosts/">eia.gov/electricity/generatorcosts</a></li>
<li>NREL Life Cycle Assessment Harmonization project, lifecycle GHG emissions for natural gas, solar PV, wind and nuclear (g CO₂-eq/kWh). Cite the specific harmonization papers you used.</li>
<li>New Hampshire 2024 capacity factors (gas 40.4%, solar 31.8%, wind 36.8%): U.S. EIA, <a href="https://www.eia.gov/electricity/generatorcosts/">eia.gov/electricity/generatorcosts</a>.</li>
<li>Natural gas land use (0.343 acres/MW): U.S. EIA, <a href="https://www.eia.gov/electricity/generatorcosts/">eia.gov/electricity/generatorcosts</a>.</li>
</ol>
<h2>How AI was used</h2>
<p class="note">I used an AI assistant to (1) find and cross-check capacity factors, land use, lifecycle emissions and cost sources, (2) set up the replacement formulas, (3) run the five scenarios, and (4) build this page. I then recalculated each result by hand and prompted it to question what the numbers leave out (see the caveats above). Extend it: add storage, change the mix, swap in offshore wind, or use another plant's MW and TWh.</p>
</main>
<script>
const T={gas:{n:'Natural gas',cf:40.4,ac:0.343,g:486,c:1004},solar:{n:'Solar PV',cf:31.8,ac:8.9,g:40,c:1865},wind:{n:'Wind',cf:36.8,ac:0.75,g:12,c:1882}};
const M=[['cap','Capacity','GW','New capacity needed to produce the same annual electricity'],['land','Land','acres','Estimated land (solar = plant area, wind = direct footprint: not fully comparable)'],['co2','CO₂-eq','t/yr','Lifecycle emissions per year'],['cost','Capital cost','$B','Up-front capital cost only']];
let cur='cap';
const P={E:9.9,ms:40,mw:30,gn:12};
const $=i=>document.getElementById(i);
const fmt={cap:v=>v.toFixed(2)+' GW',land:v=>Math.round(v).toLocaleString()+' acres',co2:v=>v>=1e6?(v/1e6).toFixed(2)+' M t':Math.round(v/1e3)+' k t',cost:v=>'$'+(v/1e9).toFixed(2)+' B'};
function build(){
 $('tabs').innerHTML=M.map(m=>`<button data-m="${m[0]}">${m[1]}</button>`).join('');
 document.querySelectorAll('#tabs button').forEach(b=>b.onclick=()=>{cur=b.dataset.m;draw()});
}
function scen(sh){let r={cap:0,land:0,co2:0,cost:0};const E=P.E*1e6;
 for(const k in sh){const t=T[k],mwh=E*sh[k],mw=mwh/(8760*t.cf/100);r.cap+=mw/1000;r.land+=mw*t.ac;r.co2+=mwh*t.g/1000;r.cost+=mw*1000*t.c}return r}
function draw(){
 const ms=P.ms/100,mw=P.mw/100;
 const S=[['Seabrook (now)',{cap:1.244,co2:P.E*1e6*(+P.gn)/1000,land:null,cost:null},1],
 ['Natural gas',scen({gas:1})],['Solar PV',scen({solar:1})],['Wind',scen({wind:1})],
 [`Mix ${Math.round(ms*100)}/${Math.round(mw*100)}/${Math.round((1-ms-mw)*100)}`,scen({solar:ms,wind:mw,gas:Math.max(0,1-ms-mw)})]];
 document.querySelectorAll('#tabs button').forEach(b=>b.classList.toggle('on',b.dataset.m===cur));
 const m=M.find(x=>x[0]===cur);$('chartTitle').textContent='Replacing Seabrook: '+m[1];$('chartNote').textContent=m[3]+'. Each case generates '+P.E+' TWh/yr. Mix is solar/wind/gas %.';
 const mx=Math.max(...S.map(s=>s[1][cur]||0));
 $('chart').innerHTML=S.map(s=>{const v=s[1][cur];return `<div class="row"><div>${s[0]}</div><div style="padding-right:110px"><div class="bar${s[2]?' nuc':''}" style="width:${v==null?0:Math.max(1,v/mx*100)}%"><span>${v==null?'not computed':fmt[cur](v)}</span></div></div></div>`}).join('');
 $('tbl').innerHTML='<tr><th>Case</th><th>Capacity</th><th>Land</th><th>CO₂-eq/yr</th><th>Capital</th></tr>'+S.map(s=>{const r=s[1];return `<tr><td>${s[0]}</td>`+['cap','land','co2','cost'].map(k=>`<td>${r[k]==null?'—':fmt[k](r[k])}</td>`).join('')+'</tr>'}).join('');
}
build();draw();
</script></body></html>
